{
  "fromId": "k",
  "toId": "j_glide",
  "preamble": "Vocalization of a velar stop to the palatal glide [j], usually via a fricative stage ([k] > [x] or [ɣ] > [j]). In Western Romance and in Brittonic the first element of the Latin cluster -ct- became a yod (factum > Portuguese feito, perfectus > Welsh perffaith); Old Spanish shared the stage *feito before yod + t fused into the affricate of hecho. In French, intervocalic k before a voiced and weakened to yod (pacāre > payer), and in Danish postvocalic Old Norse k lenited to [j] in the pronouns mig, dig, sig.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Lenition, Palatalization, Vocalization",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Portuguese",
      "languageFamily": "Ibero-Romance",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Latin factum 'done, deed'",
          "to": "feito /ˈfej.tu/",
          "note": "Latin -ct- > -it-; the yod is preserved (European Portuguese /ˈfɐj.tu/). Spanish carried the same stage further to hecho [ˈetʃo]."
        },
        {
          "from": "Latin noctem 'night'",
          "to": "noite /ˈnoj.tɨ/ (Portugal), /ˈnoj.tʃi/ (Brazil)",
          "note": "Inherited through Old Galician-Portuguese noite; Galician keeps the same form."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "French",
      "languageFamily": "Gallo-Romance",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Latin pacāre 'pacify'",
          "to": "Old French paier > payer [peje] 'pay'",
          "note": "Intervocalic k before a voiced and spirantized, ending as the glide written y."
        },
        {
          "from": "Latin necāre 'kill'",
          "to": "Old French noier > noyer [nwaje] 'drown'",
          "note": "Same development of intervocalic k."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Welsh",
      "languageFamily": "Brythonic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Latin perfectus 'perfect'",
          "to": "Middle Welsh perfeith > perffaith /ˈpɛrfai̯θ/",
          "note": "In Latin loans the k of -ct- became a glide in Brittonic (Proto-Brythonic *perfėθ), while t spirantized to θ."
        },
        {
          "from": "Latin lactis 'of milk'",
          "to": "llaeth /ɬai̯θ/ (South Wales) 'milk'",
          "note": "Via Proto-Brythonic *llaɨθ."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Danish",
      "languageFamily": "North Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Old Norse mik 'me'",
          "to": "mig /mɑj/",
          "note": "Postvocalic k lenited via [ɣ] to the glide [j]; likewise dig /dɑj/ 'you (obj.)'."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
